1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,744
BRAMBLE: Case open!

2
00:00:01,744 --> 00:00:05,567
OTTO: Hey! That's not fair! The cricket box is locked!

3
00:00:05,767 --> 00:00:09,938
IVY: The lock needs four digits. Digits are zero to nine.

4
00:00:10,338 --> 00:00:15,153
Today's case: a code hidden in thousands, hundreds, tens and ones.

5
00:00:15,553 --> 00:00:19,375
Place value means a digit's place shows its size.

6
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:25,525
Like at the canteen. Ten one-dollar coins make one ten-dollar note.

7
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:29,768
Mabel left a clue made of number blocks.

8
00:00:30,168 --> 00:00:33,301
Big cubes are thousands. Flats are hundreds.

9
00:00:33,701 --> 00:00:37,212
Long sticks are tens. Little cubes are ones.

10
00:00:38,012 --> 00:00:43,329
OTTO: Three cubes, five sticks, two little cubes. It's three, five, two!

11
00:00:43,729 --> 00:00:46,413
IVY: But the lock has four dials.

12
00:00:46,813 --> 00:00:49,100
BRAMBLE: Let's not guess. Let's test.

13
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,948
BRAMBLE: Can you find the hundreds? Point to them.

14
00:00:56,148 --> 00:00:59,967
There are no flats. So there are no hundreds.

15
00:01:00,867 --> 00:01:03,163
BRAMBLE: Which code opens the lock?

16
00:01:03,563 --> 00:01:08,055
A. Three, five, two, zero.

17
00:01:08,255 --> 00:01:12,560
B. Three, zero, five, two.

18
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:14,373
Pause and think.

19
00:01:18,973 --> 00:01:22,419
Put each block in its place. Three thousands.

20
00:01:22,819 --> 00:01:25,675
No hundreds, so we write zero.

21
00:01:26,075 --> 00:01:29,142
Five tens. Two ones.

22
00:01:29,542 --> 00:01:32,615
Zero holds the hundreds place. It's B!

23
00:01:33,015 --> 00:01:35,313
OTTO: Oh! The zero was hiding!

24
00:01:35,713 --> 00:01:40,590
Three thousand. Three thousand and fifty. Three thousand and fifty-two.

25
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:44,226
OTTO: Three, zero, five, two.

26
00:01:44,626 --> 00:01:46,122
BRAMBLE: Case solved!

27
00:01:47,622 --> 00:01:49,572
IVY: Look! A real cricket!

28
00:01:49,772 --> 00:01:52,077
OTTO: Not that kind of cricket!

29
00:01:52,977 --> 00:01:56,799
Remember. Zero holds a place when there are none.

30
00:01:57,199 --> 00:02:00,641
Say it with me. Zero holds the place.

31
00:02:02,441 --> 00:02:07,031
Try it at home. Make a secret code with a zero.

32
00:02:07,831 --> 00:02:10,808
Next case: counting acorns to ten.

